      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234518/assets/26b1e5acba63b8ee11d35e12bf0c8fd6944b7a466c9eab258ef36f6429e8ec0a.png)\n\nWhat is the radius of a sphere whose volume is $320\\pi {\\text{ cm}}^{3}?$",
      "worked_solution": "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $V=320\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 320\\pi  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 320\\pi \\cdot \\frac{3}{4\\pi } & ={r}^{3} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =240.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt[3]{240}=2\\sqrt[3]{30}\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$",
